give a CoreFoundation halt its whole stack

A HALT reported one frame, the immediate caller, and that is almost always a CF internal:
__CFBasicHashAddValue for every collection insert, __CFStringCollectionCopy for every string one. So
the report said a collection had been given a NULL and could not say who gave it one.

Measured on Money Manager EX, where the one line version was

  CoreFoundation HALT in __CFTypeCollectionRetain, called from __CFBasicHashAddValue+0x487

and the whole stack names the culprit in the same run:

  3 wxFontRefData::SetFont(__CTFont const*)
  5 wxOSXSystemFontsCacheModule::Get(wxOSXSystemFont)
  7 wxWindow::DoSetWindowVariant(wxWindowVariant)
  8 wxNonOwnedWindow::Create(...)

which is a missing UI font eleven frames away.

UNCONDITIONAL, not env gated. The process is a line away from kill(getpid(), 9), so there is nothing
to spend and no switch anyone would have remembered to set before the crash they did not expect.

--- a/CFBase.c
+++ b/CFBase.c
@@ -35,6 +35,7 @@
 #include <malloc/malloc.h>
 #include <mach/mach.h>
 #include <dlfcn.h>
+#include <execinfo.h>
 #endif
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <string.h>
@@ -895,6 +896,21 @@
         fprintf(stderr, "CoreFoundation HALT at %s:%d in %s, called from %p\n",
                 file, line, func, caller);
     }
+    /*
+     * AND THE WHOLE STACK, unconditionally. The immediate caller is almost always a CF internal
+     * (__CFBasicHashAddValue for every collection insert), so on its own it says the collection was
+     * given a NULL and not who gave it one. The process is about to die, so there is nothing to
+     * spend and no switch worth honouring.
+     */
+    {
+        void *frames[24];
+        int count = backtrace(frames, 24);
+        char **names = backtrace_symbols(frames, count);
+
+        for (int i = 1; i < count && names != NULL; i++)
+            fprintf(stderr, "CoreFoundation HALT   %s\n", names[i]);
+        free(names);
+    }
     fflush(stderr);
 }
 
